{"role":"assistant","content":"## Verdict: REVISE\n\nThe regenerated counts and reported test results are consistent, and the host `clojure.core/meta` marker guard is preserved. However, several binding Redo requirements remain unmet.\n\n### 1. Descriptor validation accepts forged and copied descriptors\n\n`kernel-callable-descriptor?` checks only:\n\n- `:clj/type` equals `:function`\n- entry is a `Closure`\n- admission is a `Closure`\n\nat `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1937-1947`.\n\nThat does **not** validate the admission relationship implemented by the compiler. The real admission check recomputes the witness from the canonical descriptor and compares it with the published witness (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2093-2107`). Consequently:\n\n- A descriptor copied wholesale from a genuine closure passes.\n- A fabricated map combining arbitrary genuine closures as entry and witness passes.\n- Removing or changing `:clj/outcomes?` also passes, despite the publisher always emitting `:clj/outcomes? true` (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1119-1127`).\n\nThe positive control itself constructs exactly such a structurally forged descriptor from two unrelated closures (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2075-2078`). The negative controls cover only missing admission, a scalar entry, a wrong type marker, and a bare map; they do not cover copied descriptors, mismatched witnesses, or a missing/false outcomes marker.\n\n### 2. Declaration revalidation is incomplete\n\n- **The declared calls are never checked.** `kernel-host-value-deviation` receives only an ID and outcomes, not the fixture (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1949-1955`). The `:call` values in `slice9_declarations.edn:12,19` are unused. There is no mutated-call negative control for either after-QED deviation.\n- **Declared boundary, upstream value, and reason are trusted rather than revalidated.** They are copied directly into the result at `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1987-1991`. A stale or tampered declaration can therefore change the recorded disposition metadata while still classifying successfully.\n- **The upstream-failure declarations are entirely unused.** `kernel-upstream-failure` still hardcodes calls, boundaries, reasons, and revision (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1994-2032`) instead of consuming `[:upstream-failures ...]` from the versioned declaration file. Thus the declarations file is not the explicit classification input for all four declared dispositions.\n- **The shape control is incomplete.** `vec` normalization at `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1968-1969` permits a same-value script whose top-level collection changes between vector and list. The `'()` control changes both payload and shape, so it does not prove that a shape-only mutation is refused.\n\nA declaration-guard failure also produces `:mismatch`, not literally `:blocked`, through the status ordering at `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2142-2146`, contrary to the new declaration comment; it still blocks the overall gate, but the documentation is imprecise.\n\n### 3. Negative controls are missing required axes\n\nIn addition to the descriptor gaps above, the controls at `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2068-2116` lack:\n\n- mutated-call coverage for the portability declarations;\n- copied-descriptor and mismatched-witness refusal;\n- malformed descriptor with missing/false `:clj/outcomes?`;\n- same-payload collection-shape-only mutation;\n- incomplete **B lane** coverage. Only a blocked walker is tested; the implementation happens to require all four completed lanes, but the brief asks for an incomplete-lane regression and the control does not exercise each lane.\n\nThe existing upstream-failure controls were retargeted correctly and include mutated ID, call, and C payload (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2342-2355`).\n\n### 4. Writer round-trip is acceptable\n\n`test-kernel-gate-evidence-writer-round-trip` genuinely invokes `write-kernel-gate-evidence!` against a temporary directory and rereads its output (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2374-2401`). Stubbing `kernel-gate-evidence` avoids rerunning fixtures but does not bypass serialization. Whole-evidence equality covers declared dispositions, statuses, raw comparisons, and coverage; ordinary gate tests remain read-only.\n\n### 5. Narrative edits are neither fully accurate nor compliant\n\nEach location was edited once, but:\n\n- `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md:143-150` still claims **145 retained arities**, **360 credited paths**, **1,879 assertions**, and **missing 20 arities**. These contradict the required 107/58 accounting and supplied 1,689-assertion run.\n- `docs/agents/known-gaps.md:352` says **“H3-0 closed the bookkeeping”**, violating the explicit instruction never to claim H3-0 is closed.\n- The new gate narrative also says forged shapes refuse (`slice9_gate.md:20-22`), which is stronger than the implementation actually guarantees.\n\n**Required before acceptance:** implement genuine descriptor/witness validation with copied/malformed controls; pass the fixture into deviation classification and compare declared calls and fixed boundary metadata; drive upstream failures from the declarations file; add the missing call/shape/lane controls; and remove both stale narrative claims and the H3-0 closure claim."}
